1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,812
Welcome to Module Twenty-Two, the last module of the unit on retrieval and memory.

2
00:00:05,812 --> 00:00:09,900
Every field biologist learns this lesson the hard way.

3
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:11,325
You will not remember it.

4
00:00:11,325 --> 00:00:17,262
Not the date, not the weather, not the strange behaviour you were sure you'd never forget.

5
00:00:17,262 --> 00:00:19,112
So you write it in the notebook.

6
00:00:19,112 --> 00:00:25,287
And the herbarium keeps the voucher specimen, with its label, long after the collector has retired.

7
00:00:25,287 --> 00:00:32,950
External memory is what lets an expedition's knowledge outlive the expedition, and lets someone else check it, decades later.

8
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:35,437
Agents need exactly the same thing.

9
00:00:35,437 --> 00:00:38,212
Think of agent memory in three layers.

10
00:00:38,212 --> 00:00:41,125
The first is working memory, the context window.

11
00:00:41,125 --> 00:00:43,650
Everything the model can attend to right now.

12
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:49,712
It's limited, and when it fills up, the model gets worse and starts forgetting your early instructions.

13
00:00:49,712 --> 00:00:52,262
And when the session ends, it's gone.

14
00:00:52,262 --> 00:00:54,425
The second layer is compaction.

15
00:00:54,425 --> 00:00:59,262
When the window fills, the older part of the conversation is replaced by a summary.

16
00:00:59,262 --> 00:01:05,050
That keeps the session going, but it loses detail, and it loses more every time it happens.

17
00:01:05,050 --> 00:01:07,637
The CMU course tells a good story here.

18
00:01:07,637 --> 00:01:12,175
One research group built a compaction method that looked perfect on a benchmark.

19
00:01:12,175 --> 00:01:19,100
Then, in real use, their agent kept forgetting it had already opened a pull request, and opened the same one five times.

20
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:22,000
Benchmarks can hide memory failures.

21
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,750
The third layer is external memory: files and databases that survive between sessions.

22
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:31,137
The agent reads and writes them with its tools.

23
00:01:31,137 --> 00:01:34,162
One of today's sources sums it up in a single line.

24
00:01:34,162 --> 00:01:36,925
If it isn't in a file, it doesn't exist.

25
00:01:36,925 --> 00:01:41,950
The people building second brains with Claude Code have converged on a simple design.

26
00:01:41,950 --> 00:01:43,525
Plain text notes.

27
00:01:43,525 --> 00:01:46,587
One topic per file, named for what's in it.

28
00:01:46,587 --> 00:01:48,425
A few clear folders.

29
00:01:48,425 --> 00:01:53,675
And one index file at the top that tells the agent where everything lives and how to behave.

30
00:01:53,675 --> 00:01:55,737
Answer only from my notes.

31
00:01:55,737 --> 00:01:56,875
Cite the note.

32
00:01:56,875 --> 00:01:59,662
And if the answer isn't there, say so.

33
00:01:59,662 --> 00:02:01,825
They also make big promises.

34
00:02:01,825 --> 00:02:03,187
Perfect memory.

35
00:02:03,187 --> 00:02:05,762
A system that gets smarter on its own.

36
00:02:05,762 --> 00:02:10,475
Treat those as hypotheses, because here's where the herbarium metaphor fails.

37
00:02:10,475 --> 00:02:12,187
A specimen is fixed.

38
00:02:12,187 --> 00:02:14,837
Nobody can quietly re-press the plant.

39
00:02:14,837 --> 00:02:17,462
Agent memory is rewritten by the agent.

40
00:02:17,462 --> 00:02:23,837
Every time it files a note, summarises, or updates a fact, a model is deciding what to keep.

41
00:02:23,837 --> 00:02:30,625
An early misreading can be stored, cited back to you as evidence, and reinforced every time it's found.

42
00:02:30,625 --> 00:02:35,162
So keep the raw originals, never let the agent delete, and audit it.

43
00:02:35,162 --> 00:02:36,312
That's the lab.

44
00:02:36,312 --> 00:02:47,437
You'll build your capstone's field notebook, with a map file and two commands: one that files your raw notes into tidy, linked pages, and one that answers questions from them with citations.

45
00:02:47,437 --> 00:02:51,525
Then you'll test its recall with ten questions you already know the answers to.

46
00:02:51,525 --> 00:02:53,925
Two of them aren't covered by your notes at all.

47
00:02:53,925 --> 00:02:56,562
The question is whether your notebook admits it.

48
00:02:56,562 --> 00:02:59,200
Open the reading, and start your notebook.

